#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163040} :activity/id #uuid "d215dc31-fcec-5081-8994-43caf3b19295" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050911 :topic/id #uuid "70a63868-505f-50a9-bb34-96c96024a1cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3138} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163310 :tutorial/content "The table below describes the number of classes taken per semester and the average final grade in a sample of four college students.\n\n| **Number of Courses** | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Average Grade** | $80$ | $85$ | $75$ | $72$ |\n\nWe can collect this data in a matrix so that each column reports the number of courses and the average grade of a single student:\n\n![](images/8a/8a489e90c10dbb65c2f5e005c32677696073cc63203116ed07d445a91d7f2d32.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **observation matrix.**\n\nSuppose we want to determine the mean number of courses and the mean average grade of this sample. The **sample mean vector,** denoted $\\mathbf m,$ is a vector containing these values. The sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom our matrix above, we have that  \n\n![](images/45/4549af29f9356865dc9f3edb67960da00a4007a71281778d4a273a1a6eb0dbf9.svg)\n\nTherefore, the sample mean vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 80\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 85\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 312\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the average number of courses is $4,$ and the average final grade is $78.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "652defa1-c802-5dbd-9ed9-7d863bd020a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6079 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e325102-149c-5fb3-b355-d46756b13060" :step/math-academy-id 36980 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162094 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161907 :question/id #uuid "5f949e10-0d73-50c8-9f41-73a0d0f78e29" :question/math-academy-id "e-9833"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f930dfb2-a688-5dbe-9233-09dbcd2ba383" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512954}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sample Mean of an Observation Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5ccead82-f536-5ecc-ad47-7e72852fc8a0" :step/math-academy-id 36981 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162095 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161908 :question/id #uuid "bd9fe016-cd5f-5e65-b388-35725ac0fbf4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9834"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc84d72a-b9f3-5f99-aa23-31cfe6910d49" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536884}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b20cf4dd-9e6f-553b-bdb8-e16bac07f0be" :step/math-academy-id 36982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163311 :tutorial/content "Let's consider again the mean-deviation matrix for the students' data we found previously:\n\n![](images/1f/1f5440b7ae08ffd19bdae816854c236afe3ca254d5bd23e26f15e34cd9c9feb2.svg)\n\nCalculating the product $B B^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 98\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's analyze what we've obtained:\n\n- The entry in the first row and first column (${\\color{red}2}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the number of courses.\n\n- The entry in the second row and second column (${\\color{blue}98}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the final grades.\n\n- The off-diagonal entries (${\\color{purple}-10}$) are the sum of the products of the mean deviations.\n\nSince there were $4$ observations in total, if we divide each entry by $4-1=3,$  we will get a matrix $C$ with the *sample variances* in the diagonal and the *sample covariance* in the off-diagonal positions:\n\n![](images/7d/7d0216b462e1bf7dc34d638a7a0de30fcf063ce39d1865b45f85b526ba7bd0a8.svg)\n\nThis matrix is called the **covariance matrix** of the data.\n\nIn general, the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations." :tutorial/id #uuid "53d475a6-4449-56eb-8cf7-736ae440e1a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6080 :tutorial/title "The Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f03f6d4f-4aba-584a-86ea-090a017828ca" :step/math-academy-id 36983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162096 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161909 :question/id #uuid "bdef2fdc-0a38-5bfa-9bc6-7ca50e8c10ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-9835"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e91708c9-67e2-5228-84fd-1f4ea60064ac" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536875}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean-Deviation Form of an Observation Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a74161b0-4fa2-5cab-a830-1e6a769934fd" :step/math-academy-id 36984 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163321 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we studied data that reported the number of courses selected and the average grades of a sample of four college students. The observation matrix for this data was\n\n![](images/9b/9bdfe848443d0014461cc51e2decfbed8dc151a72edb34825dd33ac964ed6b2f.svg)\n\nThe individual observations are\n\n![](images/3f/3f935ba5f9adea7f75a63642837a3b993286f9535d5bf1842ce3a2267413c6bc.svg)\n\nWe also found the sample mean vector \n\n![](images/26/261e1d746aafa95ac2f7a6d1ff36a741ff5ae37ba738f75b451a542baed1c5fc.svg)\n\nOne thing we can do with this data is to check how much each observation differs from the mean values. To do that, we compute the difference between each observation and the sample mean vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 80\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 85\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={x}_{4}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe collect these differences in a new matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/831e698a632d40ab447c75a3b169e29c6ae8ab10c6600d838bd699a38e837931.svg)\n\nThe matrix $B$ is the **mean-deviation form** of $X.$ It's often easier to work with the mean-deviation form, especially if the original data contains large numbers.\n\nNotice that the mean of a mean-deviation form equals the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{B} & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fcfec935-52c3-571d-b471-9a1be7383ff6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6143 :tutorial/title "Mean-Deviation Form"} :step/id #uuid "8e871f51-8d02-5990-9d6d-f5e78d503fb5" :step/math-academy-id 37301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163044}}] :activity/title "The Sample Covariance Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162232}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536938} :activity/id #uuid "e9f30801-05f6-5733-bfd9-7a227c36223a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050911 :topic/id #uuid "70a63868-505f-50a9-bb34-96c96024a1cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3138} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c32cbe8-c57b-5bf3-a19d-03d660a7ac0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}34 & -17 \\\\ -17 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536351} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba53bf9-6d2f-55fc-bcb0-4c3a502a927e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}34 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536406} {:answer/id #uuid "99ee16dd-f5ce-5d55-9f57-ac836de565b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536530} {:answer/id #uuid "a35b201c-f9c8-5ef5-99f4-35d4c0535194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}46 & 77 \\\\ 77 & 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536547} {:answer/id #uuid "f3949942-a93b-5278-9bdf-682422d7e809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536674} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef71c07-ba83-5d64-938a-f5aa72763b99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5926666e-0db3-5dee-9762-40fbc13f71e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138627" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0346e43f-4ec3-54fd-a36a-83c457ba540d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313c180d-6683-5895-8319-1cc5e1d0047d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}62 & -69 \\\\ -69 & 242\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536360} {:answer/id #uuid "3528ecab-4caa-5dc2-8341-f72254cce85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 47\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536366} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4801ac-f3c0-54c9-9d9b-068066aba1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}52 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 250\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536409} {:answer/id #uuid "551ede1c-3223-5e2a-a363-e21869ad5829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}65 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 340\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536431} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8daa35-c661-577d-b35d-32e5b20ab32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 69\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536360} :answer-field/id #uuid "677e14b0-94a5-52de-a477-ccd697d060de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f2cb4c0-5e13-5762-90d1-d095feb4a65e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138631" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -6 & 5 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -6 & 5 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}62 & -69 \\\\ -69 & 242\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23e83d25-72e7-5ce0-8291-4514123ca24b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e278261-12a2-522a-865f-2ee4f991690d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536414} {:answer/id #uuid "95c87fe0-b27d-523b-b97f-283a3892f373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536522} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6e45b4-0d19-5a65-9da0-878c4f37d052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536562} {:answer/id #uuid "c24f6fe3-8f3b-5e4f-87ee-cd603ef7bb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536590} {:answer/id #uuid "c4625cbc-5b19-59bd-9eb5-2085cc809466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536562} :answer-field/id #uuid "9980a067-163c-5305-b9d5-aeb44a51e2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9e3535a-6a10-557b-a90a-dcbee5f059a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138608" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6d56aefc-11ae-57d8-922f-7a3f39b19dcc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57509c8b-6b98-51aa-9762-9963caf04358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536435} {:answer/id #uuid "581f54a2-60c9-5dc5-bb4d-f8f318fe2d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536440} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4429f4-c191-50e8-be52-084fe635f47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536488} {:answer/id #uuid "bfecac8a-fa87-5d62-a946-7f122c1e66c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536585} {:answer/id #uuid "f1218c8e-974e-52d6-b624-68fd43f78159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536585} :answer-field/id #uuid "b518b34a-8d6e-54eb-93d9-d3cb5a0505b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a4bb009-1382-5d80-b122-5cf6749f1552" :question/math-academy-id "q-138615" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c3d9e78e-168e-5b62-9c51-d4a45ca0bbdb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f060f18-5f38-57d0-8035-ee521e1efa10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536447} {:answer/id #uuid "63f36174-881c-57cb-888e-5b635ad28017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536456} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c88b3-2c09-54a3-99c5-91c575160cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536463} {:answer/id #uuid "82ed669f-a9bd-5e1c-8760-9d608342f7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536499} {:answer/id #uuid "e3398816-b77b-5ead-950a-9a70459413a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536447} :answer-field/id #uuid "63610fdd-4a0f-5e58-8e91-40c9b79c1b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d39bfe0e-e6e3-509a-9ccb-2651e206d7a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138639" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d14ef892-c3d1-5aad-87b3-30b8a9ceff03"}] :activity/title "The Sample Covariance Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536283}]}
